1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
We take them for granted, but they're part of our everyday lives.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Over the last 100 years, our homes have become treasure troves of technological wonders.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:18,000
From the linoleum carpets of the past, to the lazy boy recliner that takes you online from the comfort of your living room.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:24,000
From the machine that washes your dishes, to the refrigerator that does everything but.

5
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,600
From the Whirlpool bath to the wide world of Teflon,

6
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:30,940
there's no place like home in the 21st century.

7
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:32,940
So come on in and stay a while.

8
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:36,140
We're putting out the welcome mat for the world of home tech,

9
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:37,940
now on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:07,440
In 1957, Monsanto built the house of the future, made

11
00:01:07,540 --> 00:01:09,940
entirely of plastic.

12
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:14,060
That means everything in the home, from the lighting to the

13
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:16,360
dishes.

14
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:20,500
And there were push buttons that operated just about everything.

15
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:24,000
The idea never caught on, and the home was done.

16
00:01:24,001 --> 00:01:27,100
demolished 10 years later.

17
00:01:27,101 --> 00:01:29,140
It took the wrecking ball six days

18
00:01:29,141 --> 00:01:31,080
to bring the house to the ground

19
00:01:31,081 --> 00:01:35,840
because of the tenacious nature of the materials.

20
00:01:35,841 --> 00:01:38,040
But plastic houses aside, we can still

21
00:01:38,041 --> 00:01:42,180
marvel at the household wonders we have at our fingertips.

22
00:01:42,181 --> 00:01:46,060
Times change, but it seems that no matter how we choose to live,

23
00:01:46,061 --> 00:01:50,020
there is a new invention, or a machine, or a gadget,

24
00:01:50,021 --> 00:01:53,560
that helps us in our never-ending quest for comfort.

25
00:01:53,561 --> 00:01:57,600
As we embark on a new century, a new kind of technology is being

26
00:01:57,601 --> 00:02:01,600
put to use in multifunctional ways to make our homes more

27
00:02:01,601 --> 00:02:07,320
intelligent. Thanks to the innovation of computer chips,

28
00:02:07,321 --> 00:02:10,520
this intelligent home in Southern California is now a

29
00:02:10,521 --> 00:02:16,760
monument to that reality. Press a button and the

30
00:02:16,761 --> 00:02:19,000
fireplace lights up.

31
00:02:19,001 --> 00:02:24,000
Skylights open.

32
00:02:24,001 --> 00:02:31,000
Security cameras located around the property can be checked in an instant.

33
00:02:31,001 --> 00:02:38,000
I believe that the question is how can we make it possible for people to live in their

34
00:02:38,001 --> 00:02:46,000
homes with less complexity, fewer things to learn and understand, no displays flashing

35
00:02:46,001 --> 00:02:50,800
anywhere in the house, and at the same time having this type

36
00:02:50,801 --> 00:02:54,800
of technology disappear entirely into the house so that you don't

37
00:02:54,801 --> 00:02:58,240
even notice it. In this house,

38
00:02:58,241 --> 00:03:01,840
keypads are strategically placed to allow the homeowner access

39
00:03:01,841 --> 00:03:05,760
to lights, heating, audio selection and levels, and a

40
00:03:05,761 --> 00:03:09,760
myriad of other functions with just the push of a button.

41
00:03:09,761 --> 00:03:12,800
The keypads themselves are inobtrusive and relatively

42
00:03:12,801 --> 00:03:16,000
simple to operate. So if it requires

43
00:03:16,001 --> 00:03:23,820
requires an instruction book or if perfectly normal everyday people feel stupid because

44
00:03:23,821 --> 00:03:28,100
they feel like they can't operate their house or operate the controls, then we as an industry

45
00:03:28,101 --> 00:03:29,100
have failed.

46
00:03:29,101 --> 00:03:36,120
It absolutely must be such that anybody who walks in the house, whether it be a homeowner,

47
00:03:36,121 --> 00:03:41,880
a child, a house guest, can go to wherever they would expect there to be a control and

48
00:03:41,881 --> 00:03:45,000
there will be a simple control there that will be obvious as to how to do what it is

49
00:03:45,001 --> 00:03:48,000
that they want to do so they can make a change to the temperature or the music.

50
00:03:49,740 --> 00:03:53,080
The outdoor waterfall is also controlled by the push of a button.

51
00:03:53,780 --> 00:03:57,600
And with the natural beauty also comes environmental efficiency.

52
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:03,540
One of the unique things about the house is that as opposed to simply dissipating the heat

53
00:04:03,541 --> 00:04:08,340
from the air conditioners into the atmosphere, which is typical, it's they're water-cooled.

54
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,260
And the heat from the heat pumps that are cooling the house, that heat is dumped into the pool.

55
00:04:14,261 --> 00:04:17,660
So the pool is, in a sense, used as a temperature sink,

56
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:18,760
if you will.

57
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:22,160
And if the pool gets too hot, it automatically

58
00:04:22,260 --> 00:04:25,100
turns on the waterfall so that we dissipate the additional heat

59
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:27,400
with evaporation.

60
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:30,300
Like the waterfall, everything in an intelligent house

61
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:32,020
is interrelated.

62
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:35,620
Thousands of wires are connected to microcontrollers that are

63
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:39,020
programmed to work both independently and together.

64
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:41,520
I think what's really driven it is the fact

65
00:04:41,521 --> 00:04:46,800
the fact that the microprocessors and the computer chips have become so affordable and

66
00:04:46,801 --> 00:04:50,492
so small that they can be built into everyday devices.

67
00:04:50,493 --> 00:04:52,680
And by having that computer chip

68
00:04:52,681 --> 00:04:57,720
in there and being able to have multiple devices somehow talk to one another, it allows you

69
00:04:57,721 --> 00:05:01,720
to have a great deal of control in the home and pretty much seamlessly.

70
00:05:01,721 --> 00:05:07,623
Here, one button will fill your indoor whirlpool to just the right temperature.

71
00:05:07,624 --> 00:05:08,520
Another will

72
00:05:08,521 --> 00:05:12,520
television for viewing pleasure while in the tub.

73
00:05:12,620 --> 00:05:16,120
And if it's privacy hereafter, these liquid crystal windows

74
00:05:16,220 --> 00:05:19,160
will go from clear to opaque in a flash.

75
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:26,180
This home is also outfitted with two home theater systems.

76
00:05:26,280 --> 00:05:28,180
To operate the unit in the bedroom,

77
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:32,180
everything can be controlled from a handheld keypad.

78
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:34,280
First, it closes the drapes.

79
00:05:34,380 --> 00:05:37,880
Then, lowers the lights.

80
00:05:37,881 --> 00:05:46,140
Go to the VCR page and press play and it works just like the kind of remote you're used to.

81
00:05:46,940 --> 00:05:51,940
And you can watch your favorite shows at any time of the day or night in the perfect environment.

82
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:55,340
It's really a bit of the Jetsons right here on Earth.

83
00:05:55,960 --> 00:05:58,900
But this intelligent technology has been a long time coming.

84
00:05:59,660 --> 00:06:04,500
Some of the early pioneers that had systems that were based on older technology used to get calls

85
00:06:04,501 --> 00:06:07,440
because the lights would just be flashing at 2 o'clock in the morning

86
00:06:07,441 --> 00:06:09,040
or the drapes would be going up and down.

87
00:06:09,560 --> 00:06:13,080
The technology has improved so much today that that typically isn't the case.

88
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:18,220
And when there is a malfunction, most often there's a way to just shut that one piece off

89
00:06:18,221 --> 00:06:19,940
or that one part that's malfunctioning off,

90
00:06:20,060 --> 00:06:22,520
and the rest of the house will continue to go on without it.

91
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:28,580
This particular home is unique in every way.

92
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:31,280
Just ask the indoor-outdoor Koi.

93
00:06:31,281 --> 00:06:35,280
But the technology is slowly becoming accessible to everyone.

94
00:06:35,281 --> 00:06:38,280
Right now there's a pretty big gap between the systems like

95
00:06:38,281 --> 00:06:42,280
what we do for the large luxury homes and what's available for

96
00:06:42,281 --> 00:06:46,280
an average consumer. And that gap is going to be reduced

97
00:06:46,281 --> 00:06:49,280
substantially by some new technologies that make the lower

98
00:06:49,281 --> 00:06:53,280
end systems more reliable and more capable. And at the same

99
00:06:53,281 --> 00:06:56,280
time, the higher end systems will become more affordable as

100
00:06:56,281 --> 00:06:58,280
well.

101
00:07:01,280 --> 00:07:04,280
It took thousands of years to get to this stage,

102
00:07:04,281 --> 00:07:06,920
but there are marvels in every room of the average house

103
00:07:06,921 --> 00:07:09,080
that we might just overlook.

104
00:07:09,081 --> 00:07:12,800
Next, we'll rest our case in the bedroom and a mattress

105
00:07:12,801 --> 00:07:15,120
that can almost make your dreams come true.

106
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:25,600
We spend one third of our lives in bed,

107
00:07:25,601 --> 00:07:27,080
but since we're asleep, perhaps we

108
00:07:27,081 --> 00:07:29,840
haven't paid much attention to the many wonders that surround

109
00:07:29,841 --> 00:07:38,080
in the boudoir, the ubiquitous alarm clock, for example.

110
00:07:38,180 --> 00:07:39,780
Over the course of time, people have

111
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:41,380
had to pay more and more attention

112
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:43,880
to being roused at a certain hour.

113
00:07:43,980 --> 00:07:49,200
And they found some very unique ways to wake up.

114
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:51,000
I'm sure you'll think of, you know,

115
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:54,040
shitting somebody in the head with a rock or chimes, you know,

116
00:07:54,140 --> 00:07:56,340
to get the monks up for prayer or whatever.

117
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:58,660
But with the development of various kind of wind-up

118
00:07:58,661 --> 00:08:02,860
mechanisms, alarm clocks, and of course the pace of modern life

119
00:08:02,861 --> 00:08:05,860
meant that people had to get up and get going to work on a

120
00:08:05,861 --> 00:08:09,900
regular basis and have it pretty well regulated.

121
00:08:14,900 --> 00:08:17,820
Depending on how you look at it, Levi Hutchins of

122
00:08:17,821 --> 00:08:20,820
Concord, New Hampshire was either the hero or the villain

123
00:08:20,821 --> 00:08:25,220
who dreamed up the first simple wind-up alarm clock in 1787.

124
00:08:25,221 --> 00:08:27,720
Many variations on the theme were developed,

125
00:08:27,721 --> 00:08:30,720
and some of the clocks boasted multiple uses.

126
00:08:30,721 --> 00:08:33,720
One would heat water and pour it for tea.

127
00:08:33,721 --> 00:08:37,220
Another would let your dog know it was time for its dinner.

128
00:08:37,221 --> 00:08:40,720
How Pavlovian.

129
00:08:40,721 --> 00:08:43,720
Some modern alarm clocks featured radios,

130
00:08:43,721 --> 00:08:47,720
and today our digital technology allows us to set our alarm clock

131
00:08:47,721 --> 00:08:49,720
to wake us up at an appointed hour

132
00:08:49,721 --> 00:08:52,720
and start the coffee maker in the kitchen.

133
00:08:52,721 --> 00:08:55,560
But getting out of bed isn't as easy these days

134
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:59,820
since we've progressed so far in mattress technology.

135
00:08:59,920 --> 00:09:04,260
Prior to springs being in a mattress and in a bed,

136
00:09:04,360 --> 00:09:07,760
you actually had ropes that crossed under the bed.

137
00:09:07,860 --> 00:09:10,780
And you had to, if you wanted to get good night's sleep

138
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:14,680
on a solid surface, you had to make sure the ropes were tight.

139
00:09:14,780 --> 00:09:16,320
And this was where our expression,

140
00:09:16,420 --> 00:09:18,720
sleep tight, originated.

141
00:09:18,820 --> 00:09:21,320
And your mattress back then was stuffed, unfortunately,

142
00:09:21,321 --> 00:09:23,820
fortunately with organic things.

143
00:09:23,821 --> 00:09:26,720
Hay, straw, horse hair.

144
00:09:26,721 --> 00:09:29,160
Now, critters lived in this stuff.

145
00:09:29,161 --> 00:09:31,940
So a few times a year when you had a nice, hot, sunny day,

146
00:09:31,941 --> 00:09:34,900
you took your mattress outside and pulled all the stuffing out

147
00:09:34,901 --> 00:09:39,040
to let it dry out and then made your bed.

148
00:09:39,041 --> 00:09:41,140
That's where the expression made your bed, it's again,

149
00:09:41,141 --> 00:09:42,900
you added the stuffing back in.

150
00:09:42,901 --> 00:09:44,640
We forget how easy we have it today.

151
00:09:47,320 --> 00:09:50,420
Coiled box springs were used as supportive foundations

152
00:09:50,421 --> 00:09:53,820
in the late 1800s. But the actual mattresses were

153
00:09:53,821 --> 00:09:59,660
still simply stuffed or tufted with material until the 1930s.

154
00:09:59,661 --> 00:10:02,060
That all changed when manufacturers started putting

155
00:10:02,061 --> 00:10:04,560
the springs right into the mattress.

156
00:10:04,561 --> 00:10:06,760
The challenge was to keep the springs from poking

157
00:10:06,761 --> 00:10:08,640
the unsuspecting sleeper.

158
00:10:08,641 --> 00:10:12,200
And a tuftless mattress was a smooth top mattress

159
00:10:12,201 --> 00:10:14,420
so that you did not have anything interfering

160
00:10:14,421 --> 00:10:17,080
with your sleep comfort on the top of the bed.

161
00:10:17,081 --> 00:10:19,220
The way they did that is we developed a way

162
00:10:19,221 --> 00:10:21,720
to attach the cover of the mattress

163
00:10:21,721 --> 00:10:24,020
and all the upholstery materials on top of the spring

164
00:10:24,021 --> 00:10:28,600
unit to the unit itself so that it wouldn't shift around.

165
00:10:28,601 --> 00:10:31,900
We think today springs is being very soft and gentle.

166
00:10:31,901 --> 00:10:37,500
But the initial springs were almost industrial size.

167
00:10:37,501 --> 00:10:40,200
And very often, they were quite dangerous.

168
00:10:40,201 --> 00:10:41,740
If you bounce too much on the bed,

169
00:10:41,741 --> 00:10:43,520
the spring popped right through and pierced

170
00:10:43,521 --> 00:10:44,440
right through your flesh.

171
00:10:44,441 --> 00:10:49,740
Engineers set out to perfect mattress inner springs so they

172
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:52,940
wouldn't poke out or otherwise annoy the user.

173
00:10:53,040 --> 00:10:54,580
But in the case of Richard James,

174
00:10:54,680 --> 00:10:57,080
another idea popped up instead.

175
00:10:57,180 --> 00:11:00,180
In 1945, James, a naval engineer,

176
00:11:00,280 --> 00:11:04,000
was experimenting with making more pliable mattress springs.

177
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:06,900
During the process, he ended up accidentally inventing

178
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,100
something that proved even more enduring.

179
00:11:10,200 --> 00:11:12,800
And he showed the thing to his wife

180
00:11:12,801 --> 00:11:16,740
to his wife, and his wife Betty said, that should be a toy.

181
00:11:16,741 --> 00:11:18,640
And she sat down with a dictionary and went through

182
00:11:18,641 --> 00:11:20,580
until she came up with the word slinky.

183
00:11:20,581 --> 00:11:23,840
And most people don't know that back in the Vietnam War

184
00:11:23,841 --> 00:11:28,880
in the late 60s, soldiers went to Vietnam with slinkies

185
00:11:28,881 --> 00:11:30,880
to use them as makeshift antennas

186
00:11:30,881 --> 00:11:33,320
to throw the slinky up into a tree.

187
00:11:33,321 --> 00:11:35,620
God knows how many trees throughout Vietnam

188
00:11:35,621 --> 00:11:37,520
have slinkies still in them to this day.

189
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:42,800
As the mattress is improved in support and comfort,

190
00:11:42,801 --> 00:11:47,060
The challenge was to convince the public that sleeping on springs would be a good idea.

191
00:11:47,580 --> 00:11:54,380
As early as the 1930s, Serta would hold demonstrations to prove the bounce-back qualities of their Tuftless product.

192
00:11:55,220 --> 00:11:59,220
We actually took a Serta Perfect sleeper and we ran a steamroller over it,

193
00:11:59,460 --> 00:12:02,300
and it came right back to its original height.

194
00:12:02,660 --> 00:12:04,160
They've been thrown out of airplanes.

195
00:12:05,300 --> 00:12:07,720
There are a number of different things that have been done to them.

196
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:09,800
I've seen people park cars on top of them.

197
00:12:09,801 --> 00:12:14,000
An Interspring mattress is a very resilient product,

198
00:12:14,001 --> 00:12:16,300
and it comes back to its original height.

199
00:12:18,200 --> 00:12:21,200
In the 1950s, mattress manufacturers

200
00:12:21,201 --> 00:12:23,180
started to promote the product as a way

201
00:12:23,181 --> 00:12:25,160
to build good posture in children.

202
00:12:26,040 --> 00:12:28,280
One of the biggest problems were

203
00:12:28,281 --> 00:12:31,040
children always got hand-me-down mattresses.

204
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:34,740
But the Sammy Serta campaign was really designed

205
00:12:34,741 --> 00:12:37,400
to help educate people that children,

206
00:12:37,401 --> 00:12:40,560
particularly children, need a good mattress,

207
00:12:40,820 --> 00:12:43,440
because it's the years when their body grows.

208
00:12:45,420 --> 00:12:48,260
As better inner spring technology was developed,

209
00:12:48,620 --> 00:12:50,740
the mattress manufacturers also boasted

210
00:12:50,741 --> 00:12:54,080
that beds were a good place to actually do physical exercise.

211
00:12:54,860 --> 00:12:56,580
It was called the Great Awakening Program,

212
00:12:57,120 --> 00:12:58,700
and it was designed to show people

213
00:12:58,701 --> 00:13:01,020
how they could do physical fitness exercises

214
00:13:01,021 --> 00:13:03,980
in their mattress, whether they were leg bends

215
00:13:03,981 --> 00:13:06,220
or they were modified sit-ups,

216
00:13:06,221 --> 00:13:08,500
and there were some things where you hung off the end of the bed.

217
00:13:08,600 --> 00:13:10,400
It was an interesting program.

218
00:13:11,460 --> 00:13:14,420
That idea proved to be a bit of a stretch for the public

219
00:13:14,421 --> 00:13:15,840
and never really caught on.

220
00:13:16,700 --> 00:13:20,140
In the 1960s, scientific tests were run

221
00:13:20,141 --> 00:13:24,960
that concluded that people would turn as many as 60 times each night while sleeping.

222
00:13:25,980 --> 00:13:29,820
This information led to a new way of constructing spring mattresses

223
00:13:29,821 --> 00:13:32,020
that would revolutionize the industry.

224
00:13:32,500 --> 00:13:34,420
Well, the first thing that they realized was

225
00:13:34,421 --> 00:13:37,820
is that the blood flow in the elbows, shoulders, and hips,

226
00:13:37,920 --> 00:13:41,720
the pressure points of the body, would be slowed down

227
00:13:41,820 --> 00:13:43,660
because the surface was too hard.

228
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:45,760
And that would make you move.

229
00:13:45,860 --> 00:13:49,360
And so the more you move, the more restless your sleep.

230
00:13:51,980 --> 00:13:53,780
In response, the mattress industry

231
00:13:53,880 --> 00:13:56,580
started using a form of convoluted foam

232
00:13:56,680 --> 00:14:00,120
that had an egg carton pattern to allow air to circulate

233
00:14:00,220 --> 00:14:01,920
under the body and help the blood flow

234
00:14:02,020 --> 00:14:04,420
at the pressure points.

235
00:14:04,421 --> 00:14:06,660
And while that increased comfort,

236
00:14:06,760 --> 00:14:10,400
Serta also developed a continuous wire mattress base

237
00:14:10,500 --> 00:14:12,720
to provide more support.

238
00:14:12,820 --> 00:14:16,200
Instead of individual springs linked together in the mattress,

239
00:14:16,300 --> 00:14:19,640
the continuous wire ran from head to toe,

240
00:14:19,740 --> 00:14:23,140
also to alleviate the pressure points of the body.

241
00:14:23,240 --> 00:14:26,500
In the late 70s, we started experimenting

242
00:14:26,600 --> 00:14:29,340
with a different type of construction,

243
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:31,940
which was really designed to solve the problems

244
00:14:31,941 --> 00:14:35,440
problems that most customers have with their mattresses.

245
00:14:35,540 --> 00:14:37,840
And one of those is sagging.

246
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:42,420
So what we were able to do is put more steel in the center

247
00:14:42,520 --> 00:14:45,420
third of the mattress unit, where you're heaviest.

248
00:14:48,520 --> 00:14:51,060
The Innerspring continuous coil technology

249
00:14:51,160 --> 00:14:53,320
has proven to be very effective in enhancing

250
00:14:53,420 --> 00:14:56,860
the support of a mattress and its longevity.

251
00:14:56,960 --> 00:14:59,900
The interesting thing about coil technology

252
00:14:59,901 --> 00:15:07,080
is that steel comes as a rod, and it's a thick rod about the size of your finger.

253
00:15:07,460 --> 00:15:11,560
And they put it through a die, and they start pulling it.

254
00:15:12,160 --> 00:15:15,020
And as they pull it, it stretches the steel,

255
00:15:15,560 --> 00:15:19,580
and they get it down to the size of wire that we need to use.

256
00:15:20,340 --> 00:15:25,620
It is then formed into a spiral, and it's shot with electricity.

257
00:15:25,621 --> 00:15:30,500
And what the electricity does is actually, it's called stress relieving.

258
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:36,200
But what it does is give a memory to that coil so that when its weight is applied to it,

259
00:15:36,240 --> 00:15:37,820
it will always come back to the same height.

260
00:15:39,600 --> 00:15:44,540
The latest in mattress technology includes a new type of foam that was developed for

261
00:15:44,541 --> 00:15:46,600
NASA and is temperature sensitive.

262
00:15:47,260 --> 00:15:53,600
As your body gives off temperature or heat, it will actually conform to your body and

263
00:15:53,601 --> 00:15:55,340
help in pressure point reduction.

264
00:15:55,620 --> 00:15:59,260
And we use that in a lot of our products today.

265
00:15:59,360 --> 00:16:01,120
And temperature regulation seems

266
00:16:01,220 --> 00:16:04,360
to be in the future of bedding technology.

267
00:16:04,460 --> 00:16:07,960
We're working with some new products that actually work

268
00:16:08,060 --> 00:16:11,260
to regulate temperature in the mattress

269
00:16:11,360 --> 00:16:15,080
so that as you give off heat, it will absorb the heat.

270
00:16:15,180 --> 00:16:17,280
And as you need it, it will give it back.

271
00:16:17,380 --> 00:16:20,420
And it will be so great because my wife's feet won't get cold

272
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:21,220
anymore.

273
00:16:21,221 --> 00:16:28,600
For the past 70 years, folks with cold feet could sleep under an electric blanket.

274
00:16:28,601 --> 00:16:33,600
The first such device was invented around 1912 and was used as a medical tool.

275
00:16:33,601 --> 00:16:41,440
It was only as big as a small paper towel, and it was put over the chest of a person

276
00:16:41,441 --> 00:16:44,387
in a sanitarium suffering from tuberculosis.

277
00:16:44,388 --> 00:16:47,200
Electricity was new at that time, and they

278
00:16:47,201 --> 00:16:50,640
They thought not only would the heat be good for your tuberculin

279
00:16:50,641 --> 00:16:54,800
cough, but the electricity would have amazing curative properties

280
00:16:54,801 --> 00:16:58,440
and go into your chest and make you better.

281
00:16:58,441 --> 00:17:01,380
They slowly got larger, but they were very dangerous because they

282
00:17:01,381 --> 00:17:03,660
were completely flammable.

283
00:17:03,661 --> 00:17:06,420
And left undetended, I mean, the entire thing on your chest

284
00:17:06,421 --> 00:17:09,960
would go up into flames.

285
00:17:09,961 --> 00:17:13,200
William Kearsley, an engineer from General Electric, is

286
00:17:13,201 --> 00:17:16,160
credited with developing the first safe blanket to hit the

287
00:17:16,161 --> 00:17:18,920
sheets in 1936.

288
00:17:18,921 --> 00:17:21,400
Native bomber pilots wore heated flight suits that

289
00:17:21,401 --> 00:17:24,300
applied the same technology.

290
00:17:24,301 --> 00:17:28,400
And in the oh-so-modern 1950s, the idea of totally warm

291
00:17:28,401 --> 00:17:30,600
tootsies really caught on with the public.

292
00:17:33,620 --> 00:17:36,120
And the commercials of the time reflect the lengths that the

293
00:17:36,121 --> 00:17:38,220
marketers would go to to prove their point.

294
00:17:41,860 --> 00:17:45,560
Another marvelous reason to roll over and stay in bed.

295
00:17:45,561 --> 00:17:47,100
But too late.

296
00:17:47,101 --> 00:17:50,000
Next, it's time to wake up and make the coffee

297
00:17:50,001 --> 00:17:52,060
in the kitchen of your dreams.

298
00:17:52,061 --> 00:17:53,960
You'll still be floating on cloud nine.

299
00:18:00,600 --> 00:18:03,380
Here's a glimpse into the future.

300
00:18:03,381 --> 00:18:06,340
A smart refrigerator that doesn't require a master's

301
00:18:06,341 --> 00:18:08,780
degree to operate.

302
00:18:08,781 --> 00:18:11,020
This screen fridge is being introduced

303
00:18:11,021 --> 00:18:13,160
in the new millennium for consumers

304
00:18:13,161 --> 00:18:15,120
that want a grocery shop from the convenience

305
00:18:15,121 --> 00:18:16,360
of their own home.

306
00:18:16,460 --> 00:18:19,560
Just scan the product in the built-in barcode scanner,

307
00:18:19,660 --> 00:18:22,460
and it's registered and then emailed to the store.

308
00:18:22,560 --> 00:18:24,660
But even more exciting than that,

309
00:18:24,760 --> 00:18:26,160
along with all the other functions

310
00:18:26,260 --> 00:18:28,660
that the Frigidaire Screen Fridge can do,

311
00:18:28,760 --> 00:18:31,700
is the whole area of home control.

312
00:18:31,800 --> 00:18:33,880
And with this area of home management,

313
00:18:33,980 --> 00:18:38,980
now you can operate and diagnose your major, other major

314
00:18:39,080 --> 00:18:43,080
appliances, and things like your security system, lighting

315
00:18:43,081 --> 00:18:47,720
systems, sprinkler systems, and so forth, household management

316
00:18:47,820 --> 00:18:50,280
through a central part of your home,

317
00:18:50,380 --> 00:18:52,460
such as the kitchen and the fridge,

318
00:18:52,560 --> 00:18:55,320
but also from afar, from your desktop at work,

319
00:18:55,420 --> 00:18:57,700
from your cell phone, maybe even from your palm pilot.

320
00:19:01,160 --> 00:19:03,160
The kitchen has always been the busiest

321
00:19:03,260 --> 00:19:06,980
room of the house, and the one that gets cleaned the most

322
00:19:07,080 --> 00:19:07,980
often.

323
00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:10,880
So it isn't surprising that many of its most modern wonders

324
00:19:10,881 --> 00:19:13,620
revolve around cleaning devices.

325
00:19:13,720 --> 00:19:17,520
In 1923, an ad appeared in House and Garden magazine,

326
00:19:17,620 --> 00:19:20,180
featuring the title, Overcoming the Drudgery

327
00:19:20,280 --> 00:19:21,420
of the Dishcloth.

328
00:19:21,520 --> 00:19:24,520
Why can't something be done to modernize

329
00:19:24,620 --> 00:19:28,140
this monotonous, everyday task?

330
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:29,940
It was touting the latest household

331
00:19:30,040 --> 00:19:32,840
wonder, the dishwasher, an invention that

332
00:19:32,940 --> 00:19:34,640
was a long time coming.

333
00:19:34,740 --> 00:19:37,340
It was originally the brainchild of Josephine Cochran

334
00:19:37,440 --> 00:19:39,140
in 1886.

335
00:19:39,141 --> 00:19:43,040
She was a wealthy society woman in Shelbyville, Illinois.

336
00:19:43,041 --> 00:19:45,440
Her husband had business and was in politics,

337
00:19:45,441 --> 00:19:47,940
and she apparently gave grand dinner parties

338
00:19:47,941 --> 00:19:51,180
and had expensive china, and would get very irritated

339
00:19:51,181 --> 00:19:53,180
at her servants when they would break her dishes

340
00:19:53,181 --> 00:19:55,980
or chip them after a nice meal.

341
00:19:55,981 --> 00:19:57,800
So she finally gave her servants the order

342
00:19:57,801 --> 00:19:59,400
that only she would wash her own dishes,

343
00:19:59,401 --> 00:20:01,300
and then, of course, she decided she didn't like

344
00:20:01,301 --> 00:20:04,000
washing dishes all that much, and had to invent

345
00:20:04,001 --> 00:20:06,200
a dishwasher to take care of that brow.

346
00:20:06,201 --> 00:20:09,800
So she started monkeying around in her garage

347
00:20:09,801 --> 00:20:12,760
and hammering together a unit

348
00:20:12,761 --> 00:20:14,860
to be able to figure out how to do this.

349
00:20:17,520 --> 00:20:19,880
Cochran's first incarnation of this device

350
00:20:19,881 --> 00:20:21,360
used a hand pump system

351
00:20:21,361 --> 00:20:23,400
that basically threw water at the dishes.

352
00:20:24,140 --> 00:20:26,040
Eventually, she added a steam generator

353
00:20:26,041 --> 00:20:27,140
to run the contraption,

354
00:20:27,141 --> 00:20:29,740
but it was only used in large hotels

355
00:20:29,741 --> 00:20:32,800
or gatherings like the World's Fair in 1893.

356
00:20:33,540 --> 00:20:35,420
It took enormous amounts,

357
00:20:35,421 --> 00:20:39,820
of very hot scalding water, and this was a very dangerous device.

358
00:20:39,821 --> 00:20:41,820
Not like safe dishwashers today.

359
00:20:41,821 --> 00:20:44,220
If that door accidentally sprung open,

360
00:20:44,221 --> 00:20:46,660
everybody in the vicinity would be scalded.

361
00:20:49,160 --> 00:20:53,100
Cochrane started the Garris Cochrane Company in the late 1800s.

362
00:20:53,780 --> 00:20:57,780
But it wasn't until after World War II when the company became KitchenAid

363
00:20:57,781 --> 00:21:01,480
that dishwashers started to show up in the average American home.

364
00:21:01,481 --> 00:21:10,560
Right after the war, the first true household dishwasher that came out had a pressurized

365
00:21:10,561 --> 00:21:16,360
system where a pump would direct the water through a rotating wash arm.

366
00:21:16,361 --> 00:21:23,920
Was patented by KitchenAid in 1949, and that was called a KD-10.

367
00:21:23,921 --> 00:21:30,140
And the real key to the utility of that machine was the fact that it had two racks, a low

368
00:21:30,141 --> 00:21:34,380
rack and an upper rack that you could neatly stack the dishes

369
00:21:34,480 --> 00:21:38,240
in, the glasses, the cups, and it had a silverware basket.

370
00:21:38,340 --> 00:21:41,080
I personally feel that that was probably

371
00:21:41,180 --> 00:21:46,180
the beginning of a very, very good machine that

372
00:21:46,280 --> 00:21:49,200
was really useful in the household.

373
00:21:49,300 --> 00:21:52,600
While many women really didn't like doing the dishes,

374
00:21:52,700 --> 00:21:56,800
the machines didn't sell right away for a couple of reasons.

375
00:21:56,900 --> 00:21:58,640
And what I think those people discovered

376
00:21:58,641 --> 00:22:02,080
is that in the earlier days, women did not find the

377
00:22:02,180 --> 00:22:04,380
dishwashing to be that terrible a task.

378
00:22:04,480 --> 00:22:07,840
It was a nice time for relaxing at the end of the day.

379
00:22:07,940 --> 00:22:10,380
It did feel good to have warm water on your hands.

380
00:22:10,480 --> 00:22:13,820
It called the family together to help with the dishes.

381
00:22:16,660 --> 00:22:18,260
The makers of dishwashers started

382
00:22:18,360 --> 00:22:20,900
emphasizing the very hot temperatures of the water

383
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:22,200
in the machines.

384
00:22:22,300 --> 00:22:24,860
There were issues, of course, in the beginning,

385
00:22:24,960 --> 00:22:28,640
but the temperature and the fact that the machines did

386
00:22:28,641 --> 00:22:31,040
have, call it the sanitation effect,

387
00:22:31,041 --> 00:22:34,220
was very, very useful in convincing people

388
00:22:34,221 --> 00:22:37,240
that those machines were very good

389
00:22:37,241 --> 00:22:40,120
and that you could reach temperatures

390
00:22:40,121 --> 00:22:44,280
that you could not normally do by washing with your hands.

391
00:22:45,920 --> 00:22:49,460
For several decades, dishwasher laboratory technicians

392
00:22:49,461 --> 00:22:53,260
have painstakingly painted foodstuff onto plates and glassware

393
00:22:53,261 --> 00:22:55,300
to test the efficiency of their machines.

394
00:22:55,301 --> 00:23:00,300
And at Whirlpool, they still use the same techniques today.

395
00:23:00,400 --> 00:23:03,300
Dishwashers are put through very stringent tests

396
00:23:03,400 --> 00:23:06,120
to ensure that they can completely clean even the most

397
00:23:06,220 --> 00:23:09,320
caked-on food and meet government standards.

398
00:23:09,420 --> 00:23:13,460
Some of the soils in that standard include oatmeal,

399
00:23:13,560 --> 00:23:19,720
a raspberry, coffee grounds, egg, mashed potatoes, coffee,

400
00:23:19,820 --> 00:23:22,920
tomato juice, items of that nature.

401
00:23:22,921 --> 00:23:29,480
of that nature, and they are dried for two hours under controlled conditions of humidity

402
00:23:29,481 --> 00:23:33,636
and temperature, and I can guarantee you they become very tenacious.

403
00:23:33,637 --> 00:23:34,920
These dishes are then

404
00:23:34,921 --> 00:23:44,540
loaded in the dishwashers and washed, and then are graded for the absence of soil.

405
00:23:44,541 --> 00:23:49,480
Tests are also conducted to record the noise level that the machines generate.

406
00:23:49,481 --> 00:23:56,560
I remember the days that you could really not carry a decent conversation within six

407
00:23:56,561 --> 00:23:58,020
or eight feet of the machine.

408
00:23:58,021 --> 00:24:02,400
Because it was noisy, it was metal, and it was pumping at very high pressures.

409
00:24:02,401 --> 00:24:08,600
We really started emphasizing sound, and the first machine that was called a whisper quiet

410
00:24:08,601 --> 00:24:13,400
came out in the early 80s, and that significantly reduced the sound.

411
00:24:13,401 --> 00:24:17,400
That was at the cost of a lot of sound deadening materials.

412
00:24:20,740 --> 00:24:24,040
Yes, the dishwasher is truly a gift for the household.

413
00:24:24,140 --> 00:24:26,980
But amazingly, it still hasn't replaced the small kitchen

414
00:24:27,080 --> 00:24:31,260
wonder that Edwin Cox created in 1917.

415
00:24:31,360 --> 00:24:35,020
Cox sold aluminum pots door to door in San Francisco.

416
00:24:35,120 --> 00:24:37,120
And he wanted a gimmick to get him in the houses

417
00:24:37,220 --> 00:24:39,100
of his potential customers.

418
00:24:39,200 --> 00:24:42,260
So he took steel wool pads and encrusted them in soap

419
00:24:42,261 --> 00:24:44,160
and used them as his calling card.

420
00:24:44,560 --> 00:24:46,760
The housewives loved the pads.

421
00:24:46,840 --> 00:24:48,420
They still didn't buy his pots and pans,

422
00:24:48,600 --> 00:24:51,300
but he then went into manufacturing these little things,

423
00:24:51,320 --> 00:24:53,760
and it was his wife who came up with the name SOS

424
00:24:53,761 --> 00:24:55,820
for Save Our Sauce Pans.

425
00:24:58,320 --> 00:25:02,720
And the garbage disposal was developed by John Hammes in 1927.

426
00:25:03,500 --> 00:25:05,900
It took him 11 years to finish his design,

427
00:25:06,100 --> 00:25:08,040
which he called Insincarator.

428
00:25:08,320 --> 00:25:11,440
He saw a dirty, stinky mess and said,

429
00:25:11,441 --> 00:25:13,240
hey, I think I can solve this.

430
00:25:13,880 --> 00:25:16,120
But his simple device is still the basis

431
00:25:16,121 --> 00:25:17,400
for what is used today.

432
00:25:18,180 --> 00:25:20,800
He scrape food scraps off the plate into the sink.

433
00:25:20,940 --> 00:25:22,580
It washes down into the disposer,

434
00:25:23,080 --> 00:25:25,600
and there's a spinning plate inside the disposer.

435
00:25:25,940 --> 00:25:29,700
That spinning plate really throws the food out to the edges

436
00:25:29,701 --> 00:25:31,080
using centrifugal force.

437
00:25:31,700 --> 00:25:35,000
Along the edges, there's kind of a rough surface,

438
00:25:35,200 --> 00:25:38,680
and the food is just beaten up and ground into little parts.

439
00:25:39,120 --> 00:25:41,020
Contrary to popular belief, there are no blades

440
00:25:41,021 --> 00:25:44,060
in a food waste disposer.

441
00:25:44,061 --> 00:25:47,620
But even without blades, the disposer lives up to its nickname,

442
00:25:47,621 --> 00:25:50,020
the electric pig.

443
00:25:50,021 --> 00:25:54,260
In the test lab, they go through all sorts of test protocols.

444
00:25:54,261 --> 00:25:56,660
But the one that's the most impressive, I think, to people

445
00:25:56,661 --> 00:26:00,580
is they put 35 pounds of frozen steer rib bones.

446
00:26:00,581 --> 00:26:03,180
And for all purposes, it's like throwing 35 pounds of rocks

447
00:26:03,181 --> 00:26:04,780
into the disposer.

448
00:26:04,781 --> 00:26:07,580
It sounds horrendous, but it won't hurt the disposer.

449
00:26:07,581 --> 00:26:11,580
But one of the biggest breakthroughs in kitchen

450
00:26:11,680 --> 00:26:15,580
technology was discovered by accident at DuPont Laboratories.

451
00:26:15,680 --> 00:26:19,520
In 1938, scientist Roy Plunkett was experimenting with

452
00:26:19,620 --> 00:26:23,620
refrigeration gases when he noticed a white, waxy substance

453
00:26:23,720 --> 00:26:25,640
that had been left as a residue.

454
00:26:25,740 --> 00:26:29,040
He tested the material and found that it had some extremely

455
00:26:29,140 --> 00:26:31,640
unique qualities. One of the

456
00:26:31,740 --> 00:26:34,080
characteristics of this, it's the slipperiest substance

457
00:26:34,180 --> 00:26:36,140
known to man. The coefficient of friction

458
00:26:36,141 --> 00:26:37,540
friction is incredibly low.

459
00:26:37,640 --> 00:26:40,440
In fact, it's like wet ice rubbing against wet ice.

460
00:26:43,240 --> 00:26:45,380
Teflon is actually the trademark name

461
00:26:45,480 --> 00:26:48,380
for polytetrafluorethylene.

462
00:26:48,480 --> 00:26:51,180
It is a substance that is inert and does not react

463
00:26:51,280 --> 00:26:54,700
with anything else, which gives it its slippery quality.

464
00:26:54,800 --> 00:26:56,820
And this elusive substance has managed

465
00:26:56,920 --> 00:26:59,700
to slip into some pretty important areas of our lives.

466
00:27:02,440 --> 00:27:04,940
For one, the Statue of Liberty.

467
00:27:04,941 --> 00:27:10,680
Even she has experienced the joys of Teflon, which is used as an insulator and anti-corrosive

468
00:27:10,681 --> 00:27:14,360
between her copper skin and her stainless steel skeleton.

469
00:27:15,460 --> 00:27:20,940
And besides making us the land of the rust free, Teflon has many other space age applications.

470
00:27:22,020 --> 00:27:26,860
Teflon has actually gone to the moon, it's gone to Mars, it's gone to Jupiter as part of the space program.

471
00:27:27,380 --> 00:27:30,640
There we've lined some of the moon rock bags are lined with Teflon.

472
00:27:30,641 --> 00:27:34,660
A lot of the space suits have Teflon on them.

473
00:27:34,940 --> 00:27:38,280
And many of the components that go to make up a spacecraft,

474
00:27:38,580 --> 00:27:41,680
the seals and bearings, have Teflon there also.

475
00:27:43,920 --> 00:27:47,580
Teflon wasn't used on cookware until the early 1960s.

476
00:27:47,820 --> 00:27:51,420
The problem was how to get a substance that doesn't stick to

477
00:27:51,421 --> 00:27:53,420
anything to stick to something.

478
00:27:54,220 --> 00:27:57,740
And what's happened is that we discovered a way to apply a

479
00:27:57,741 --> 00:28:02,680
primer coat to the pans that allows the bottom layer of that

480
00:28:02,681 --> 00:28:06,720
primer coat to stick to the pan in the top layer is the PTFE,

481
00:28:06,820 --> 00:28:07,520
the Teflon.

482
00:28:07,820 --> 00:28:09,160
And that was the key invention.

483
00:28:09,260 --> 00:28:12,620
But that took a good 15 years to figure out how to do that and

484
00:28:12,720 --> 00:28:14,120
make it work consistently there.

485
00:28:15,660 --> 00:28:18,500
After all that kitchen cleanup, it's time to relax.

486
00:28:27,840 --> 00:28:31,240
In the late 1800s, bathroom chic meant having a nicer

487
00:28:31,241 --> 00:28:31,941
outhouse.

488
00:28:31,942 --> 00:28:37,040
But since the introduction of indoor plumbing, the bathroom has slowly evolved into

489
00:28:37,041 --> 00:28:40,125
one of the most comfortable and livable rooms in the house.

490
00:28:40,126 --> 00:28:42,060
One of the first improvements was the

491
00:28:42,061 --> 00:28:48,260
unparalleled indoor privy. It became the seat of power in the early 1900s.

492
00:28:48,261 --> 00:28:49,600
It was only when

493
00:28:49,601 --> 00:28:53,980
plumbing started coming into homes and people stopped using their outhouses and their chamber

494
00:28:53,981 --> 00:28:58,560
pots that they started to install these beautiful porcelain flush toilets.

495
00:28:58,561 --> 00:28:59,860
And there was, by the

496
00:28:59,861 --> 00:29:02,456
way, a man named Thomas Crapper.

497
00:29:02,457 --> 00:29:07,000
Crapper did not invent the crapper, but he was a Briton,

498
00:29:07,001 --> 00:29:14,120
and he lived in the 1890s, and he was, coincidentally, a plumber, and he made many improvements on

499
00:29:14,121 --> 00:29:17,051
the flush mechanisms in toilets.

500
00:29:17,052 --> 00:29:22,180
While bathrooms were becoming more popular in the house,

501
00:29:22,740 --> 00:29:27,960
actual regular bathing was a concept that most people found distasteful in the early 19th century.

502
00:29:27,961 --> 00:29:34,180
There were very wealthy who would once a month, maybe once every six months, get in a tub

503
00:29:34,181 --> 00:29:35,880
and fill it up with water.

504
00:29:35,881 --> 00:29:39,940
But even then, the entire family would do this on one night, and the water would simply

505
00:29:39,941 --> 00:29:41,320
be reused.

506
00:29:41,321 --> 00:29:45,040
Mom would get in first, then she'd get out, and the dad would get in the dirty water,

507
00:29:45,041 --> 00:29:49,080
then the kids would get in the dirty water, and this was it for six months.

508
00:29:49,081 --> 00:29:56,120
In the early 1920s, plumbing manufacturers wanted to capitalize on their wares, so they

509
00:29:56,121 --> 00:30:00,960
started to emphasize bathing as a way to promote better health.

510
00:30:00,961 --> 00:30:05,160
The plan worked, and hygiene and cleanliness became de rigueur,

511
00:30:05,161 --> 00:30:07,860
and the bathroom became a room to be reckoned with.

512
00:30:07,861 --> 00:30:11,160
Advances in medical science were coming around to realizing

513
00:30:11,161 --> 00:30:13,980
that there was something transmittable that could be

514
00:30:13,981 --> 00:30:16,280
prevented with some kind of cleanliness.

515
00:30:16,281 --> 00:30:18,980
So hand washing became more important,

516
00:30:18,981 --> 00:30:20,620
toilet facilities and so forth.

517
00:30:20,621 --> 00:30:25,480
And as hot and cold water could be delivered to households,

518
00:30:25,481 --> 00:30:28,120
it became more important to be aware of those things.

519
00:30:28,220 --> 00:30:30,180
And then, of course, nicer homes,

520
00:30:30,860 --> 00:30:32,160
lovelier kitchens and bathrooms,

521
00:30:32,280 --> 00:30:35,420
all the style that went along with that kind of functionality

522
00:30:35,520 --> 00:30:36,460
became popular.

523
00:30:37,900 --> 00:30:40,200
Bathtub designs became the fashion.

524
00:30:40,300 --> 00:30:42,860
One Victorian space-saving design

525
00:30:42,960 --> 00:30:45,540
included a folding bathtub that could be stored away

526
00:30:45,640 --> 00:30:46,600
in a wooden cabinet.

527
00:30:47,900 --> 00:30:50,440
Showers were invented that could shoot needles of water

528
00:30:50,540 --> 00:30:51,480
all over the body.

529
00:30:51,580 --> 00:30:55,300
Very much a forerunner of today's modern jet-propelled tubs

530
00:30:55,301 --> 00:31:01,000
showers. Today, bathtubs can simulate

531
00:31:01,001 --> 00:31:04,900
waterfalls, river flow, and tidewater.

532
00:31:11,440 --> 00:31:14,720
The luxury whirlpool tubs that are commonplace in the bathroom

533
00:31:14,721 --> 00:31:20,720
today were first conceived by Roy Jacuzzi in 1968.

534
00:31:20,721 --> 00:31:23,460
Jacuzzi came from a long line of inventors.

535
00:31:23,461 --> 00:31:26,660
His grandfather and six brothers came to America from Italy in

536
00:31:26,661 --> 00:31:30,760
the early 1900s, and very soon after invented a new pitch

537
00:31:30,761 --> 00:31:33,500
propeller for airplanes.

538
00:31:33,501 --> 00:31:36,800
They also devised a covered monoplane and delivered mail

539
00:31:36,801 --> 00:31:39,000
to remote areas of California.

540
00:31:39,001 --> 00:31:42,820
The Jacuzzi brothers then went into the area of crop irrigation

541
00:31:42,821 --> 00:31:45,220
and invented a new kind of injection pump to help

542
00:31:45,221 --> 00:31:50,120
distribute the water from the deep wells into the fields.

543
00:31:50,121 --> 00:31:52,660
In the 1950s, one member of the family

544
00:31:52,661 --> 00:31:55,900
got rheumatoid arthritis, and the brothers created a special

545
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:59,200
device for him using one of their pump systems.

546
00:31:59,300 --> 00:32:02,600
In the old days, they called it water treatments,

547
00:32:02,700 --> 00:32:05,200
in which they would literally take a pump.

548
00:32:05,300 --> 00:32:06,780
It looked like an outboard engine,

549
00:32:06,880 --> 00:32:08,440
and stick it in a bathtub.

550
00:32:08,540 --> 00:32:11,780
And it would circulate the water around your body.

551
00:32:11,880 --> 00:32:15,920
This is what primarily was provided in the hospitals,

552
00:32:16,020 --> 00:32:17,760
not really something that you'd want

553
00:32:17,860 --> 00:32:22,020
to do with an electric motor on the bathtub.

554
00:32:22,021 --> 00:32:24,260
When Roy Jacuzzi got out of college,

555
00:32:24,360 --> 00:32:27,400
he went to work on a design that would still circulate the water

556
00:32:27,500 --> 00:32:31,300
in the bathtub, but in a safer, more therapeutic way.

557
00:32:31,400 --> 00:32:33,640
He worked on his invention for over a year.

558
00:32:33,740 --> 00:32:36,840
My idea was primarily to develop the bathtub

559
00:32:36,940 --> 00:32:40,300
with the jets, which is actually injecting air and water

560
00:32:40,400 --> 00:32:44,980
into the system, and make it so you have a multitude of jets

561
00:32:45,080 --> 00:32:47,380
around the perimeter of the design.

562
00:32:47,480 --> 00:32:51,880
So it gave you, instead of one source of just circulating

563
00:32:51,881 --> 00:32:56,280
I induced air and water through a fairly sophisticated Venturi

564
00:32:56,380 --> 00:32:59,120
system that I could develop adjustment to.

565
00:33:01,460 --> 00:33:04,000
G.B. Venturi, an Italian physicist

566
00:33:04,100 --> 00:33:06,600
in the 18th century, devised the system

567
00:33:06,700 --> 00:33:11,540
Roy Jacuzzi's grandfather used in his agricultural jet pumps.

568
00:33:11,640 --> 00:33:16,280
There's a hole, and it looks like a very slight V

569
00:33:16,380 --> 00:33:20,000
in which water is compressed to the back of it.

570
00:33:20,001 --> 00:33:23,240
And as it goes through the V, it explodes.

571
00:33:23,340 --> 00:33:27,700
What I did was I induced a hole on the top and also the bottom,

572
00:33:27,800 --> 00:33:31,620
allowing the atmosphere or the air to be injected in

573
00:33:31,720 --> 00:33:36,220
as it was exploding, thus causing a tremendous amount of volume

574
00:33:36,320 --> 00:33:38,080
of water as it explodes.

575
00:33:38,180 --> 00:33:41,760
And as it hits your body, the air and water would explode,

576
00:33:41,860 --> 00:33:46,400
giving you true hydrotherapy and make your body relax

577
00:33:46,500 --> 00:33:47,200
tremendously.

578
00:33:50,000 --> 00:33:52,440
The bathroom would never be the same.

579
00:33:54,420 --> 00:34:02,500
In the 1950s, people used to envision a day when the bathroom would be a total environment for relaxation, suited to any mood.

580
00:34:03,240 --> 00:34:07,300
With the invention of the whirlpool baths and showers, that day has arrived.

581
00:34:08,140 --> 00:34:15,260
Today's tubs and showers are equipped with CD players, VCRs, and, alas, Internet capabilities.

582
00:34:15,261 --> 00:34:20,800
After a nice relaxing shower, most of us have to dry our hair.

583
00:34:21,460 --> 00:34:27,040
And in the annals of home tech, hair dryers first evolved through very unlikely channels.

584
00:34:27,680 --> 00:34:31,160
The first hair dryer originated in the 1860s.

585
00:34:31,200 --> 00:34:32,580
It was by a hairdresser.

586
00:34:32,800 --> 00:34:33,780
You might expect that.

587
00:34:33,840 --> 00:34:35,440
His name was Alexander Godfried.

588
00:34:35,860 --> 00:34:41,580
And his idea was to put a little plastic bonnet on the head, connect it with a flexible tube

589
00:34:41,581 --> 00:34:46,580
to a gas stove or your fire-powered stove

590
00:34:46,820 --> 00:34:49,680
and to suck off the exhaust heat

591
00:34:49,681 --> 00:34:51,700
and dry your hair with the exhaust.

592
00:34:51,701 --> 00:34:54,460
The next real invention with the hair dryer

593
00:34:54,461 --> 00:34:56,760
was in the 1920s, and this was a result

594
00:34:56,761 --> 00:34:58,400
of the vacuum cleaner.

595
00:34:58,401 --> 00:35:00,460
Women started using vacuum cleaners,

596
00:35:00,461 --> 00:35:02,200
and you know when you use a vacuum,

597
00:35:02,201 --> 00:35:04,500
there's hot air blown out the back.

598
00:35:04,501 --> 00:35:06,140
So the manufacturer decided,

599
00:35:06,141 --> 00:35:08,800
well, let's not waste this hot air.

600
00:35:08,801 --> 00:35:10,540
Let's see if we could come up with an idea,

601
00:35:10,541 --> 00:35:12,540
and they decided to sell the vacuum cleaner

602
00:35:12,541 --> 00:35:14,840
with a flexible hose and a little hat.

603
00:35:14,841 --> 00:35:17,580
You put the hat on, you turn your vacuum cleaner on

604
00:35:17,581 --> 00:35:20,080
to dry your hair, and you took the exhaust out.

605
00:35:23,580 --> 00:35:25,780
The pistol grip hair dryer was first developed

606
00:35:25,781 --> 00:35:28,140
in the early part of the 20th century

607
00:35:28,141 --> 00:35:31,140
and lasted for more than 40 years.

608
00:35:31,141 --> 00:35:34,440
And then over time, of course, in the 50s, 60s, 70s,

609
00:35:34,441 --> 00:35:36,140
hairstyles changed, and hair dryers

610
00:35:36,141 --> 00:35:37,840
had to change along with them.

611
00:35:37,841 --> 00:35:41,840
So when the bouffant hairdos were popular,

612
00:35:41,841 --> 00:35:44,840
big rollers and bonnet hair dryers became popular,

613
00:35:44,841 --> 00:35:47,340
and then the hard hat bonnet kind of hair dryers

614
00:35:47,341 --> 00:35:49,240
that replicated the kind of things that were used

615
00:35:49,241 --> 00:35:51,840
in beauty parlors also were popular.

616
00:35:51,841 --> 00:35:54,000
And then, of course, if you recall the 70s,

617
00:35:54,001 --> 00:35:55,840
people were wearing long and flowing hair,

618
00:35:55,841 --> 00:35:57,040
and so they weren't gonna sit around

619
00:35:57,041 --> 00:35:58,600
under bonnet hair dryers anymore.

620
00:35:58,601 --> 00:36:02,180
And so they needed a pistol grip hair dryer shape again.

621
00:36:04,880 --> 00:36:06,300
No matter how you cut it,

622
00:36:06,301 --> 00:36:09,500
The bathroom has taken on a mysterious seductiveness

623
00:36:09,501 --> 00:36:11,840
that is only getting more amazing.

624
00:36:11,841 --> 00:36:14,140
But it's a personal room, a private place.

625
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:22,140
If you're looking around for another household wonder,

626
00:36:22,141 --> 00:36:23,960
it's right under your feet.

627
00:36:23,961 --> 00:36:26,740
In modern homes, we take resilient, no-wax flooring

628
00:36:26,741 --> 00:36:28,220
for granted.

629
00:36:28,221 --> 00:36:30,340
It's a fixture in many kitchens.

630
00:36:30,341 --> 00:36:33,620
It's relatively inexpensive and easy to clean.

631
00:36:33,621 --> 00:36:37,520
But in the late 1800s, linoleum carpets were considered

632
00:36:37,521 --> 00:36:38,520
a luxury item.

633
00:36:45,100 --> 00:36:47,300
The process of making linoleum starts

634
00:36:47,301 --> 00:36:50,800
with oxidized linseed oil.

635
00:36:50,801 --> 00:36:53,680
The oil is then mixed with resin.

636
00:36:53,681 --> 00:36:56,100
The addition of limestone and cork dust

637
00:36:56,101 --> 00:36:58,380
turned the substance into a type of cement.

638
00:36:58,381 --> 00:37:05,380
The linoleum cement is then applied to a jute backing.

639
00:37:05,381 --> 00:37:14,380
The linoleum sheets are put into heated drying chambers where they mature for several weeks

640
00:37:14,381 --> 00:37:17,380
and develop their permanent wear characteristics.

641
00:37:17,381 --> 00:37:25,380
And you produce this miraculous floor that took a beating, didn't stain, and today, I mean, we sort of scoff at linoleum,

642
00:37:25,381 --> 00:37:27,380
It's something cheap to put on your floors.

643
00:37:27,381 --> 00:37:32,380
But in the 1870s and 1880s, it was so expensive and so new

644
00:37:32,381 --> 00:37:34,380
that only the wealthy could use it,

645
00:37:34,381 --> 00:37:37,380
and they would roll up these priceless oriental carpets

646
00:37:37,381 --> 00:37:41,380
and get rid of them to put down these incredible linoleum floors.

647
00:37:41,381 --> 00:37:44,380
You put it in your parlor, you put it in your living room,

648
00:37:44,381 --> 00:37:48,380
the fanciest room, and you had people over to see your linoleum carpeting.

649
00:37:50,380 --> 00:37:52,380
Thomas Armstrong heard about the process

650
00:37:52,381 --> 00:37:57,380
formulated in England in the 1860s. Armstrong ran a cork

651
00:37:57,381 --> 00:38:01,380
factory and was intrigued by the idea of using cork shavings to

652
00:38:01,381 --> 00:38:04,380
create a new product. So he built a factory to produce

653
00:38:04,381 --> 00:38:09,380
the floor covering in Lancaster, Pennsylvania in 1926.

654
00:38:09,381 --> 00:38:12,380
Armstrong also had the bright idea of adding colors and

655
00:38:12,381 --> 00:38:16,380
patterns to the mix, and the trend caught on in a big way in

656
00:38:16,381 --> 00:38:21,380
the early 20th century. Armstrong had the genius to

657
00:38:21,381 --> 00:38:25,160
to really make the patterns interesting,

658
00:38:25,420 --> 00:38:27,580
introduced geometric patterns,

659
00:38:27,720 --> 00:38:29,560
and mass produced it in quantity,

660
00:38:29,561 --> 00:38:31,240
which, of course, brought down the price

661
00:38:31,241 --> 00:38:35,160
such that the average person then could start installing it in one's home.

662
00:38:35,380 --> 00:38:37,960
By that time, it had already lost favor with the wealthy,

663
00:38:38,080 --> 00:38:40,800
and they had pulled their oriental carpets back out of storage

664
00:38:40,801 --> 00:38:42,540
and put them down on their hardwood floors.

665
00:38:45,140 --> 00:38:48,600
Linoleum is produced today in much the same manner as the last century,

666
00:38:48,601 --> 00:38:52,660
but now with the aid of computers for testing and quality control.

667
00:38:54,660 --> 00:38:57,420
And the flooring is having a resurgence of sorts,

668
00:38:57,720 --> 00:39:01,000
with new designer patterns and uses in all rooms of the home.

669
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:06,920
But with all that floor space, we needed some appropriate furniture.

670
00:39:07,680 --> 00:39:11,920
Perhaps one of the most beloved and long-lasting household wonders of the 20th century

671
00:39:11,921 --> 00:39:15,140
is the comfy chair known as the Lazy Boy.

672
00:39:15,141 --> 00:39:22,320
The concept was crystallized in 1929 by cousins Ed Knabusch and Ed Shoemaker in Monroe, Michigan.

673
00:39:22,860 --> 00:39:26,020
Their first creation was a wood slat chair that could recline.

674
00:39:26,700 --> 00:39:30,660
But its seasonal nature as an outdoor chair made it a hard sell.

675
00:39:31,020 --> 00:39:34,740
So the men set about making an upholstered chair that reclines.

676
00:39:35,180 --> 00:39:36,500
And a legend was born.

677
00:39:36,501 --> 00:39:42,660
Once they introduced their first automatic reclining chair,

678
00:39:42,661 --> 00:39:51,240
They called it just the first automatic reclining chair, and they said, wait a minute, we'll have to come up with a name for this.

679
00:39:51,720 --> 00:39:54,480
How about having a contest with our few employees?

680
00:39:55,100 --> 00:39:59,100
And they did, and they offered them a prize for the favorite name.

681
00:39:59,620 --> 00:40:08,960
And they came up with slack back, sit and snooze, comfort carrier, and someone said, lazy boy.

682
00:40:08,961 --> 00:40:16,320
And that person won a $5 or $25 prize for coming up with the name Lazy Boy.

683
00:40:18,720 --> 00:40:22,540
After only six months in business, the Great Depression hit the U.S.

684
00:40:22,541 --> 00:40:27,740
But the two Eds, as they were called, were determined to stay afloat, so they bartered for their furniture.

685
00:40:28,360 --> 00:40:33,300
So sometimes people who wanted one of their products or the other things that they sold

686
00:40:33,301 --> 00:40:38,340
would offer them perhaps a couple of chickens or a cow

687
00:40:38,341 --> 00:40:42,900
or a load of coal for their basement to heat their furnace

688
00:40:42,901 --> 00:40:46,840
or some wheat that the local farmers had.

689
00:40:48,280 --> 00:40:51,500
During World War II, the men adapted their business

690
00:40:51,501 --> 00:40:52,920
to help out the war effort.

691
00:40:53,640 --> 00:40:56,300
They surrendered their furniture-making business

692
00:40:56,301 --> 00:41:01,100
so that someone could make aircraft parts in our building,

693
00:41:01,101 --> 00:41:05,180
and Lazy Boy moved into the basement and into some rented buildings

694
00:41:05,181 --> 00:41:11,880
to make Army tank seats, Army tank seat backs, crash pads,

695
00:41:12,320 --> 00:41:17,520
whatever other work they could get to keep going for during the war

696
00:41:17,521 --> 00:41:19,860
and also to contribute to the war effort.

697
00:41:21,620 --> 00:41:27,620
Then in the 1960s, a new Lazy Boy was designed and a star was born.

698
00:41:27,621 --> 00:41:32,620
In 1961, they introduced the Recliner Rocker.

699
00:41:32,720 --> 00:41:36,360
It was a chair that reclined and rocked

700
00:41:36,460 --> 00:41:41,160
and had its own automatic footrest.

701
00:41:41,260 --> 00:41:43,660
The chair was such a success that over the course

702
00:41:43,760 --> 00:41:48,980
of the next 10 years, sales increased by $50 million.

703
00:41:49,080 --> 00:41:51,080
While styles may have changed over the years,

704
00:41:51,180 --> 00:41:53,120
the construction of the Lazy Boy Recliner

705
00:41:53,220 --> 00:41:56,380
has stayed basically faithful to the original design.

706
00:41:56,381 --> 00:42:00,380
It still operates with the use of one wooden lever.

707
00:42:00,381 --> 00:42:02,380
Well, it's a very, very simple handle

708
00:42:02,381 --> 00:42:04,380
that's on the right-hand side of the chair,

709
00:42:04,381 --> 00:42:07,380
also available on the left-hand side.

710
00:42:07,381 --> 00:42:08,380
But we call it the comfort selector

711
00:42:08,381 --> 00:42:11,380
because once you pull that comfort selector back

712
00:42:11,381 --> 00:42:13,380
and your feet go up and the chair goes back,

713
00:42:13,381 --> 00:42:16,380
it's pure comfort.

714
00:42:16,381 --> 00:42:18,380
In spite of his old age,

715
00:42:18,381 --> 00:42:20,380
Lazy Boy has tried to keep up with the latest

716
00:42:20,381 --> 00:42:23,380
in laid-back consumer demands.

717
00:42:23,381 --> 00:42:31,660
In the 1990s, he was reinvented as the Oasis, a chair that has a built-in heat massager and a small fridge.

718
00:42:32,060 --> 00:42:36,740
So now you have fewer excuses to actually get out of the chair, even if you could.

719
00:42:37,460 --> 00:42:44,500
I think some of the stress and strains of today's world have really kept Lazy Boy in the forefront of what's comfortable in today's homes.

720
00:42:44,880 --> 00:42:49,140
And I think putting the massage and the heat function in it makes it even more so.

721
00:42:49,141 --> 00:43:00,400
And in keeping with the times, Lazy Boy is now equipped with a brain of its own in a new model that is called the e-cliner because of its internet capabilities.

722
00:43:01,300 --> 00:43:12,813
The Web TV recliner from Lazy Boy provides the user with the ability to get on the internet and really interact with their television in a much better way than regular

723
00:43:12,814 --> 00:43:13,514
TV.

724
00:43:13,515 --> 00:43:16,020
Interactive television gives you the opportunity to get in

725
00:43:16,120 --> 00:43:18,660
and play some games and also get on the Internet

726
00:43:18,760 --> 00:43:21,760
and surf your favorite spots.

727
00:43:21,860 --> 00:43:24,260
Just pretend you're Captain Kirk and beam up

728
00:43:24,360 --> 00:43:28,160
from the comfort of your living room.

729
00:43:28,260 --> 00:43:30,980
Throughout the decades, the ultimate vision for modern

730
00:43:31,080 --> 00:43:35,680
home improvements really hasn't changed that much.

731
00:43:35,780 --> 00:43:38,680
As I've looked over the last hundred years of household

732
00:43:38,780 --> 00:43:41,580
products and appliances, one thing that is very striking

733
00:43:41,581 --> 00:43:45,780
is that in the American household, the same needs recur,

734
00:43:45,880 --> 00:43:48,880
and different products solve those same needs.

735
00:43:48,980 --> 00:43:51,920
So we've always been looking for comfort, convenience,

736
00:43:52,020 --> 00:43:55,120
performance, utility function, and style.

737
00:43:55,220 --> 00:43:57,220
And so things will change in their appearance,

738
00:43:57,320 --> 00:44:01,540
but oftentimes fulfill the same function.

739
00:44:01,640 --> 00:44:04,540
